{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth","id":565768,"title":"[Beast] Help: I Don't See How Beast Deconstructs the Monomyth","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-06-23T14:51:39-07:00","id":565768,"title":"[Beast] Help: I Don't See How Beast Deconstructs the Monomyth","content":"So Beast's supposed to be a deconstruction of the monomyth, but I don't really see how it does that. I've seen some comments that it does this by killing the Hero, but that seems to me like it just confuses Heroes-the-Character-Type for Heroes-the-Narrative-Role. I should admit that I'm only familiar with the Campbellian form of the monomyth, but things still seem to line up quite well. What am I missing? Aren't Beasts just the subject of the Hero's Journey?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/985-wasteful","name":"Wasteful","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-23T15:29:12-07:00","id":565798,"title":"","content":"Beasts are a large step in the monomyth, namely the Abyss and the Threshold Guardians. However, those archetypes, when you think about them are actually really <i>constricting. </i>If you're a Threshold keeper, you exist to be overcome in a build of dramatic action and then promptly forgotten about. If you're a Abyss, all the impact you have is helping define who the Real Main Character is, which while important, also condemns you to being forgotten about and cast aside. Simply by being more than their roles and often directly objecting, Beasts cast the whole thing in a darker light.<br /> <br /> On the Heroic side of things, though, it also shows just how...<i>myopic </i>it is. It's assumed by the monomyth that the main character is ultimately the star of the show; there may be other good guys, but they're supporting roles to bow in and out as the situation requires. The thing is, that kind of attitude, put into the real world, is <i>narcissism. </i>More than that, the very fact the Hero is the main character also somewhat babies him (and I am using male pronouns quite deliberately, because the half-baked kind of monomyth Heroes embody has a lot to do with the most poisonous aspects of masculinity): because he's never called upon to make self-reflection and personal development, he never really progresses. The very fact the story's about him prevents him from following it to its ultimate conclusion, just going around in circles at steps one or two. The Hero assumes any problem can be solved via strength rather than wisdom, knowledge rather than self-awareness. About the only problems that solves are the Beasts themselves, <i>sometimes, </i>and even that's a bit iffy, because again, the Beasts are more complex than simply being the Guardians or Abysses.<br /> <br /> In effect, they're the danger of thinking in terms of monomyth without nuance, in that if you just use the monomyth itself instead of simply seeing it as a pattern or deliberately toying with it, you miss the point; the Return with the ability of the Hero to teach others to be Heroic. And a Hero who doesn't share, doesn't evolve, not because he can't, but he <i>doesn't want to</i>...<br /> <br /> Well, Doctor Doom is a villain for a reason, despite being a savior to Latveria. He can't let himself see beyond his own Beast - that would require understanding that he <i>screwed up</i> somewhere.<br /> <br /> (Speaking of, really boring superhero stories tend to make that mistake too-a criticism of the monomyth as an aspect of American culture <i>alone</i> I found outright says that a lot of the monomyth-based stories is preserving the status quo. Not always a good thing).<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also, I just found that another big thing is that the monomyth tends to confuse solving problems with personal growth. That...can be really scary sometimes (see what would happen if Orpheus, instead of realizing he missed his shot and moving on, declared war on Hades to get his wife back).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":180,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","user":"Leliel","date":". 06-23-2015, 06:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-23T15:45:11-07:00","id":565813,"title":"","content":"Oh yeah, while it's layman's analysis, <a href=\"http://roguepriest.net/2011/07/04/why-i-dont-like-joseph-campbell/\" target=\"_blank\">there's this really good article on it</a>.<br /> <br /> A commenter on there really cinched it; a truly heroic person is defined by <i>acting...</i>but the lazy way of writing out the monomyth (plus not recognizing its maker was a flawed man - not evil, just a product of his times) makes the Hero <i>react.</i><br /> <br /> Like, say, never questioning whether or not your perceptions of a Primordial Dream may lack depth and simply following them around without trying to understand what they are or where they come from.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-23T16:26:54-07:00","id":565842,"title":"","content":"A major part of The Hero's Journey story is that, after slaying the Beast, he's supposed to gain some kind of wisdom, elixer, or something that helps improve the day-to-day lives of the people. In part, that's where the save-the-princess trope comes from- they're restoring the system of order and government to people's lives.<br /> <br /> In fact, that elixer, princess*, whatever it is? The McGuffin? That's the real point of the journey, the monomyth. Heroes never really achieve that Mcguffin in the real world. There's no universal fix-all. In fact, we can argue that the mcguffin is just a stand-in for wisdom and learned lessons. And Heroes are specifically the ones who DON'T learn the lessons the Beast is trying to teach with their nightmares.<br /> <br /> In a way, Heroes are actually <i>failed heroes</i> where those who learn the lessons of fear are the ones who've successfully &quot;slain&quot; the dragon and gained the lesson-mcguffin. That's why the Heroes are low Integrity and trapped in an endless cycle, unable to go on. They bypass the pearls of wisdom for swine, and keep moving on and on, looking for the &quot;real treasure.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT - <br /> * Yes, I realize I'm reducing &quot;princess&quot; to an object here. That's the issue with the monomyth - we're dealing with abstract symbols, not real beings. A princess isn't a person, she's the governing body. I don't like reducing people to objects, but that unrealism is one of the things that's being deconstructed in the game. Realizing we're not dealing with symbols, but actual flesh and blood people","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 06-23-2015, 07:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-23T17:51:14-07:00","id":565897,"title":"","content":"Okay, but all of this just brings me back to my last question (which I'm totally going to rephrase, and so may seem super different): Doesn't the line in fact present Campbellian Heroes in the form of Beasts? If so, isn't it really reconstructing the monomyth by presenting distorted &quot;false&quot; versions alongside the genuine article?<br /> <br /> As a brief reference to what I'm talking about, here's <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monomyth\" target=\"_blank\">a link to the Wikipedia article</a>. I'm particularly thinking about everything from &quot;Meeting with the Goddess&quot; on, since &quot;The Call to Adventure&quot; to &quot;The Road of Trials&quot; is basically just a feature of RPGs in general. Apex, Hunger, Lair, and the end-states seem particularly well-suited to the Campbellian Monomyth (even more than Mage, I'd argue, which in general reminds me a lot of this era of literary criticism).<br /> <br /> Also, I really want to thank the people who have been replying here. Your comments have been very insightful, and while I know I'm largely not engaging with your arguments, that's because the version of this where I've tried have largely just obscured my actual response.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":985,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/985-wasteful","user":"Wasteful","date":". 06-23-2015, 08:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/985-wasteful","name":"Wasteful","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T01:42:30-07:00","id":566193,"title":"","content":"Not really? A Beast certainly doesn't have any specific Call To Adventure, though they do have a moment of awakening/rebirth (possibly aided by a mentor figure) that truly makes then a Begotten. Each one has her own life (and Life) with details and a myriad of possibilities that exist outside the boundaries of the monomyth.<br /> <br /> It may still exist, but their role in that, the role the world tries to force them to play when their actions slip into what humans expect of it, is not that of the adventurer/hero. When the monomyth intersects with their existence, they are drawn into the place of the monster, provided to be at best an example and at worst an obstacle. Beasts contest this, both because they are more than what the shallow model of these stories say they are, and because they were originally of much greater importance in the /proper/ takes on the tales (or so Beasts believe).<br /> <br /> They have a place in myths and stories, but it's not that of the hero. The fact is this: Beasts fight the pull of the monomyth because they are more than what it says, and they know this; because they have the awareness that going along with it is being nothing more than a slave who gives Heroes reason.<br /> <br /> (Writing this post while about to doze off, so it may not make the most sense.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T02:23:23-07:00","id":566202,"title":"","content":"Honestly it's not a Deconstruction of the Traditional Monomyth.. more on the Modern Interpretation. It is like you say a Reconstruction, Except casting the Traditional Villains as the Main cast.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Light and Dark are two Sides of a Coin... Humanity is What Happens when it Lands on it's Edge.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3981-rickmanuk","name":"RickmanUK","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3981_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"533"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T03:19:12-07:00","id":566218,"title":"","content":"Just because Heroes are flawed and mistaken campbellian Heroes doesn't make the Beast a successful one. It's not really a &quot;modern interpretation&quot; either. Beast The Primordial is a subversion of the narrative because its protagonists are in many ways the exact same figures stories cast in the role of the monstruous antagonist or malicious obstacle without caring for what they are beyond that (and these stories include more than those which fit in the monomyth model), and because it is integral to their conflict as independent characters they possess the ability to defy expectations and have their stories outside of the usual narrative, despite what they truly are.<br /> <br /> If the book is a deconstruction of the monomyth, it's in the sense that it criticizes it. The Begotten defy its restrictive and straightforward generalizations, and Heroes in its cosmology, especially in the context of their relation to the Primordial Dream, contest the assumptions of the structure of the monomyth, as well as critique the shallowness that the persona of the hero so often possesses, reduced to solving issues through violence and little else, even if that's not the only thing that may be in the tale.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T04:47:37-07:00","id":566247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>RickmanUK</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566202#post566202\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly it's not a Deconstruction of the Traditional Monomyth.. more on the Modern Interpretation. It is like you say a Reconstruction, Except casting the Traditional Villains as the Main cast.</div> </div> </div> </div> Maybe people are having problems with this question because they misunderstand what deconstruction means? <br /> <br /> Deconstruction means to nothing more than an in depth analysis of the story. Naturally, since this is a game, we analyze the myth through playing it. We play it by having a reinterpretation of the story as the setting. In this way, its a deconstruction, no matter if it was a subversion, reconstruction, or modern interpretation, simply because the analysis is a natural consequence of playing the game. That's all there is to it. We're looking at the different parts of the monomyth as they come up in play.<br /> <br /> Its not like we're talking about opposite terms here. Deconstruct, modern interpretation, subversion, and reconstruction are not opposites here, they're not mutually exclusive terms. Honestly, they're all part of the same thing.<br /> <br /> I really hate reducing things down to dictionary definitions, but that's what it sounds like to me. A misunderstanding over the terms used. We can't really have a discussion if we're all misunderstanding what each other say. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T05:58:26-07:00","id":566285,"title":"","content":"I think some people (possibly including one or more of the authors at a few points) are conflating deconstruction with subversion. I think Beast can certainly do both, but it's more geared toward the deconstruction, examining and questioning the components of the expected narrative beyond the simplistic tropes that make them up.<br /> <br /> Of course depending on the campaign, it probably subverts the monomyth frequently as well, otherwise the PCs would all be dead.<br /> <br /> (Personally, I think 'the monomyth' as a thing is a bunch of pretentious wankery, but that's a whole different conversation.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sisyphus has it easy...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1647-mechalith","name":"Mechalith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1647_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T06:24:19-07:00","id":566292,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mechalith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566285#post566285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(Personally, I think 'the monomyth' as a thing is a bunch of pretentious wankery, but that's a whole different conversation.)</div> </div> </div> </div> Even if it is, the idea of a Chosen Hero fighting against an Evil Monster IS highly prevalent in stories. Take D&amp;D and all derivatives - its pretty much built on the concept. Take stories of Heracles, of Thor, of <span style=\"color:#545454\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Susanoo, </span></span>and other monster slayers. <br /> <br /> Even if the monomyth itself is bunk, the idea of the monster slayer, fantasy racism, etc are all still valid subjects to be analyzed. I consider BtP to be the WoD equivalent to fantasy games, and an analysis of how the &quot;evil races&quot; are seen as opposed to the &quot;goodly characters.&quot; <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T06:39:34-07:00","id":566301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566292#post566292\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Even if it is, the idea of a Chosen Hero fighting against an Evil Monster IS highly prevalent in stories. Take D&amp;D and all derivatives - its pretty much built on the concept. Take stories of Heracles, of Thor, of <span style=\"color:#545454\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Susanoo, </span></span>and other monster slayers.<br /> <br /> Even if the monomyth itself is bunk, the idea of the monster slayer, fantasy racism, etc are all still valid subjects to be analyzed. I consider BtP to be the WoD equivalent to fantasy games, and an analysis of how the &quot;evil races&quot; are seen as opposed to the &quot;goodly characters.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I absolutely agree, didn't mean to imply that I don't. There's a ton of good story fodder and inspiration to play off in any number of existing legends and games, and the overall concept of deconstructing the expected narrative has a lot of potential.<br /> <br /> I'm not a fan of the whole monomyth/Hero's Journey thing because it feels like someone trying to pass off &quot;if you generalize hard enough, everything starts to sound the same&quot; as a profound revelation to me. I may be a bit prejudiced against it because the person who first introduced me to the concept was an Olympic level crystal waver. After the thousandth time you hear someone say 'It really makes you <i>think</i>, huh?&quot; you just start wanting to beat them with a stapler. (Or I do, anyway.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Sisyphus has it easy...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1647-mechalith","name":"Mechalith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1647_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"364"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T06:51:21-07:00","id":566306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mechalith</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566301#post566301\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not a fan of the whole monomyth/Hero's Journey thing because it feels like someone trying to pass off &quot;if you generalize hard enough, everything starts to sound the same&quot; as a profound revelation to me. I may be a bit prejudiced against it because the person who first introduced me to the concept was an Olympic level crystal waver. After the thousandth time you hear someone say 'It really makes you <i>think</i>, huh?&quot; you just start wanting to beat them with a stapler. (Or I do, anyway.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Chosen Blade (Stapler) sounds like the <i>best</i> signature weapon. Previously it was the stun-gun wielded by Rhonda Blank in &quot;The Morning After&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T07:04:30-07:00","id":566315,"title":"","content":"Well, the monomyth, in my opinion, can easily be described as &quot;basic storytelling structure.&quot; You have an introduction, challenges, a climax, and then a conclusion; no surprise, since the monomyth is a literary analysis of stories, not a cultural interpretation. But I think we're starting to move into Changeling territory here.<br /> <br /> Despite the occasional reference to stories, I don't think that Beast is really about stories, or rather, myths and legends. Yes, you're a &quot;legendary monster&quot; modeled after those from mythology. But I feel like that's the extent of it. Beast is about choosing your own path in life, just like its about choosing your own family, and choosing what being a monster means. I suppose one could say that its all about choosing your own story instead of a pre-determined path. Its all about Nurture over Nature and self-determination.<br /> <br /> The entire monomyth thing is a distraction, because the point of the game is to deliberately defy any such classifications. Its not like Changeling, where you take up classical roles. Its where you do your level best to shred the roles into smithereens. Its where people try to tell you that you can or can't do something, and you do it anyways. Its about overcoming bullies, shrugging off cultural and peer pressures, and being your own person.<br /> <br /> Its about giving the monomyth the bird and walking off into noon day sun, because eff the sunsets, you're better than them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T07:48:45-07:00","id":566398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566247#post566247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really hate reducing things down to dictionary definitions, but that's what it sounds like to me. A misunderstanding over the terms used. We can't really have a discussion if we're all misunderstanding what each other say.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-size:12px\">That's fair. I wasn't trying to come off as claiming the definitions<i> aren't </i>all similar (they are, and they're pretty close to each other), but for the sake of clarification, this is what I meant:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:11px\">&quot;Modern Interpretation&quot;: Reusing the story elements and framework, but in a modern setting, replacing outdated forms with current ones. Romeo And Juliet is a pretty overdone example, just change the feuding noble families into rival gangs, warring mafia families, or rival family managed corporations. The monomyth in Beast to me doesn't fit in this, because its most important elements are founded on fantasy notions, rather than necessarily medieval or anachronistic ones, so work in nearly every time period. I do know this is probably not what Rick meant by it, but for the sake of clarity.<br /> <br /> Subversion: The deliberate challenging or inversion of notions common to the genre and the exploration of what it means for the story. Beast does this with its most basic element of the premise. In a game with heroes and monsters and a cycle based around their conflict, you play the monster, not the &quot;hero&quot;.<br /> <br /> Deconstruction: Taking apart the literary devices that make up a genre or type of story, critiquing them, and reassembling them in a new way, exploring the narrative in a manner that is not usually done (far as I know this definition is also used in cooking). Beast is this in how it discusses the monomyth, the relationship between Beasts and Heroes, and the standard ideas relating to both.</span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> But, as MCN said above and others (myself included) already pointed out before, one of the points of Beast is precisely in defying the regular ideas. and I would go as far as to say that one of the driving struggles of the Begotten is their fight against the classifications thrust into them as a matter of fact.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":188,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","user":"YeOfLittleFaith","date":". 06-24-2015, 12:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T08:30:04-07:00","id":566441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566247#post566247\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Maybe people are having problems with this question because they misunderstand what deconstruction means?<br /> <br /> Deconstruction means to nothing more than an in depth analysis of the story. Naturally, since this is a game, we analyze the myth through playing it. We play it by having a reinterpretation of the story as the setting. In this way, its a deconstruction, no matter if it was a subversion, reconstruction, or modern interpretation, simply because the analysis is a natural consequence of playing the game. That's all there is to it. We're looking at the different parts of the monomyth as they come up in play.<br /> <br /> Its not like we're talking about opposite terms here. Deconstruct, modern interpretation, subversion, and reconstruction are not opposites here, they're not mutually exclusive terms. Honestly, they're all part of the same thing.<br /> <br /> I really hate reducing things down to dictionary definitions, but that's what it sounds like to me. A misunderstanding over the terms used. We can't really have a discussion if we're all misunderstanding what each other say.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you are conflating experience of a thing with analysis of a thing, and that's led to a weird conclusion where constructing a structure is somehow the same as deconstructing a structure. I agree that we aren't talking about opposite or necessarily mutually exclusive terms, but we are talking about terms with different meanings. To deconstruct a work is to reveal the ways it is not complete on its own, that it is still attached to the umbilical of the transcendental signifier, and that it is standing on corpses it claims aren't there. If it was merely to recognize that there are parts to the whole, Derrida wouldn't have a problem with Saussure. He does however, because the Professor hadn't even realized how he privileged the spoken word over the written in his work on the signifier/signified relationship.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/985-wasteful","name":"Wasteful","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T09:01:59-07:00","id":566479,"title":"","content":"See, this is what I mean it is all about different definitions. You're using a definition that's different from everyone else, and then arguing from a position that relies on people agreeing on your definitions. Frankly, I'm not interested in a semantics argument, and that's all this is turning into. <br /> <br /> We've all given our thoughts on what we mean when we say &quot;deconstructing the monomyth.&quot; You don't think that's what deconstruction means? That's fine. But that doesn't change the answers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T09:31:57-07:00","id":566586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566479#post566479\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See, this is what I mean it is all about different definitions. You're using a definition that's different from everyone else, and then arguing from a position that relies on people agreeing on your definitions. Frankly, I'm not interested in a semantics argument, and that's all this is turning into.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh? You're suggesting a discussion based in lit crit, social konstructivism and other <i>'useful'</i> humanistic subject matter is turning into a tiresom grind?<br /> No way! Who would've guessed?! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1958-krobeles","name":"Krobeles","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1958_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T10:17:47-07:00","id":566638,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/565768-beast-help-i-don-t-see-how-beast-deconstructs-the-monomyth?p=566479#post566479\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See, this is what I mean it is all about different definitions. You're using a definition that's different from everyone else, and then arguing from a position that relies on people agreeing on your definitions. Frankly, I'm not interested in a semantics argument, and that's all this is turning into.<br /> <br /> We've all given our thoughts on what we mean when we say &quot;deconstructing the monomyth.&quot; You don't think that's what deconstruction means? That's fine. But that doesn't change the answers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I apologize for not specifying that I was referring to the concept of deconstruction in literary criticism. I thought it was implicit it in the discussion, since the topic was how the book deconstructs the monomyth, which is itself a concept in literary criticism. My choice to invoke deconstruction in the subject was itself mirroring a choice in the book, so I thought it was a safe term to use. Obviously, I should have been more explicit.<br /> <br /> I know doing this undercuts the above apology, but I'd like to point out that I'm not using an archaic or personal definition of deconstruction. If you google it, that's the meaning which will come up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/985-wasteful","name":"Wasteful","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T10:26:55-07:00","id":566644,"title":"","content":"Really, no need for apologies. I might be a little short myself here. Its just that these kinds of arguments get old quick, and I want to spend the time talking about the game, not definitions. I know where you're coming from, you know where I am. We're on the same page now, so lets all just enjoy talking about how Beast is or is not about about fantasy racism, or something like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-24T11:19:59-07:00","id":566713,"title":"","content":"I think Beast is not about fantasy racism, but could be. The big problem is Hunger, which enforces modes of monstrous behavior. There is room for nuance in how you address that psychological craving for hording/dominance/whatever, but that desire is still there.<br /> <br /> That's part of why I find your discussion of Changeling as being about fulfilling archetypes fascinating, because I have the exact opposite view. Changelings are forced into roles to the point where it changes their nature, but the moment they escape Arcadia, they don't have to do that any more. That the Spring Court kind of imitates the weird fairy tale masquerade lifestyle of the Keepers concerns the other Courts, even though the Spring courtiers would argue they are just trying to take control of that part of their life. The Winter Court could arguably be called &quot;The Court of Not Paying Any Attention To That Time&quot;.<br /> <br /> Beasts, on the other hand, chose to take on their roles (though some comments suggest that might be changing a bit in the revision). They can certainly grow disillusioned with that decision, but they can't really escape it. Even if a Beast joins a monastery, they will still have that Hunger. It's part of them forever. Even in the Apex (which most directly resembles the Atonement with the Father-&gt;Apotheosis-&gt;Return format of the Campbellian monomyth), it's still there. It just doesn't have as much power (I would say because they've gone through the Temptress stage, but whatever).<br /> <br /> Even worse, the Beast within kind of reinforces that behavior. Don't feed enough, and it goes hunting itself... Which at least in the original draft was the primary way Heroes formed. Is it a coincidence that a starving Beast creates entities which remind the Beast of its distance from humanity?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/985-wasteful","name":"Wasteful","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"92"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}